WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.375
Mr Albrecht had worked at the city's lost-property office for twenty-three years. He had seen everything: umbrellas in every colour, single gloves, a set of false teeth, an urn, once even a wedding dress that was never collected. For every object he wrote an index card, carefully and in fine handwriting, even though a computer had long stood on his desk.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:48.452
On a Tuesday in November, a bus driver brought in a small, worn notebook. On the first page there was no name, only one sentence: 'In case I forget who I was.' Mr Albrecht should have put it in a box as usual. Instead, he took it to the park in his lunch break and began to read.

00:00:48.702 --> 00:01:13.888
The book was full of memories, written in a shaky hand: the smell of the bakery where the writer had fetched bread rolls as a child; the name of a dog; an evening of dancing in 1961; a quarrel with a sister that had never been settled. The last pages grew shorter and shorter. On the very last one there was only an address.

00:01:14.138 --> 00:01:33.928
After work, Mr Albrecht drove to that address. It was a care home. The woman at reception looked at the notebook and fell silent. 'Mrs Keller has been looking for it for days,' she said quietly. 'She no longer knows exactly what she has lost. Only that it was important.'

00:01:34.178 --> 00:01:55.333
He sat down with the old lady in the common room. She held the book tightly in both hands, leafed through it slowly and smiled at some of the pages, as if meeting old friends. After a while she looked up and asked whether he could read something to her. He read to her until it grew dark outside.

00:01:55.583 --> 00:02:06.888
Mr Albrecht wrote no index card that evening. But the next morning he bought himself a small notebook and wrote a single sentence on the first page.
